    isbefore: []  # ac42
    duplicate_of: []  # ac42
    not_duplicate_of: []  # ac42
    pr_ref:  # ac42
    description: "FOUND 2026-08-27 while measuring WI-lutuh's rust grants. This is why those\ngrants move no verdict, and it is upstream of everything the campaign has been\nfixing.\n\nA CARGO BINARY TARGET IS NOT A CALL. The rust manifest analyzer emits an edge\nfrom a `[[bin]]` target to the entrypoint it names, TYPED `calls`, with a src\nwhose language slot is `toml`:\n\n  toml:Cargo.toml:15-19:mini-redis-cli:binary   -> rust:src/bin/cli.rs:75-146:main:function\n  toml:Cargo.toml:19-23:mini-redis-server:binary-> rust:src/bin/server.rs:32-44:main:function\n  toml:Cargo.toml:91-99:just:binary             -> rust:src/main.rs:1-5:main:function\n\n`_call_production_coverage` computes `languages_with_calls - set(catalogs)`.\nThere is no `toml.yaml` among the fifteen shipped catalogues, so `toml` lands in\nthat difference and the run reports:\n\n  \"No net_send chains found, but language(s) toml made calls but have no I/O\n   catalog, so none of their I/O could be classified; cannot confirm the\n   boundary is unused.\"\n\nCONSEQUENCE: every Rust repository that declares a `[[bin]]` target -- which is\nevery Rust binary crate -- has EVERY boundary claim withheld as inconclusive,\nno matter how complete the rust catalogue becomes. WI-lutuh's statement is that\n\"every clean Rust boundary verdict is withheld\"; the grants close the half that\nstatement names, and THIS closes nothing, because a manifest declaration is\nbeing counted as a call by a \"language\" that cannot make one.\n\nMEASURED, on the A/B cohorts: mini-redis, netavark, just and ripgrep are all\nheld by `toml`. On mini-redis it is the reported blocker for all three claims.\n\nWHAT THAT DOES *NOT* ESTABLISH, stated because the temptation is strong:\n`_call_production_coverage` is a ladder of early returns, so the reason string\nnames the FIRST blocker, not every blocker. \"toml is what mini-redis reports\"\nis therefore NOT \"toml is all that stands between mini-redis and a clean\nverdict\" -- another rung may be waiting behind it. Sizing the payoff needs the\ngate re-run with the toml edges removed, which this filing has not done.\n\n  $ python3 -c \"...survey.json...\"   # mini-redis, 895 edges\n    edges touching a toml id: 31\n       depends_on_manifest: 27\n       defines_target: 2\n       calls: 2        <- these two\n\nNote `defines_target` already exists and carries exactly this meaning. The two\n`calls` edges are a second, differently-typed home for the same relationship.\n\nFISH IS NOT THE SAME DEFECT AND MUST NOT BE FOLDED IN. `just` and `ripgrep` also\nreport `fish`, and those edges are REAL:\n\n  fish:completions/just.fish:1-6:__fish_just_complete_recipes:function\n      -> fish:external:0-0:printf:external_symbol   (also `string`, `commandline`)\n\nA fish script genuinely calls printf and genuinely can perform I/O, so\nwithholding is INV-javam working as designed. That is a missing-catalogue\nCOVERAGE GAP, not a miscategorisation. Same for perl/powershell/tcl on git.\n\nTHREE CANDIDATE FIXES, not ranked, because the choice is a modelling decision:\n  (a) Do not type a manifest target-declaration as `calls` -- `defines_target`\n      is already there and already means this. Narrowest, but touches an\n      edge-type the call graph may deliberately traverse for entrypoint\n      reachability.\n  (b) Exclude manifest-origin languages from `languages_with_calls`. Localised\n      to the gate, but adds a second place that knows what a manifest is.\n  (c) Ship a `toml.yaml` declaring no I/O surface. Cheapest, and honest -- a\n      data format performs no I/O -- but it treats a modelling artifact as a\n      catalogue gap and would need the same for every future manifest format.\n\nSIZED, AND THE RUST-COHORT VIEW OVERSTATED IT. Measured across the 42-repo\nsurvey corpus (lutuh_scope_08242026), 14 of 42 repos have at least one verdict\nwithheld by a catalogue-less language, and the population is dominated by REAL\nlanguages where withholding is CORRECT:\n\n   nix 3 · fish 3 · manifest 2 · ruby 2 · powershell 2 · lean · asm · glsl\n   · svelte · typescript · solidity · swift-objc · perl · tcl\n\n`toml` appears in exactly ONE of the 42 (ripgrep). So toml is NOT the\ncorpus-wide dominant blocker; it is systematic for SMALL RUST BINARY CRATES\nspecifically — 4 of the 13 rust repos in the A/B cohorts (ripgrep, mini-redis,\njust, netavark), where there is little else to withhold on. Both statements are\ntrue and only the second one motivates a fix.\n\n`manifest` (2 repos) is very likely the SAME CLASS under a different pseudo-\nlanguage name and should be checked in the same pass; the rest of that list is\nINV-javam working as designed and wants catalogues, not a re-typing.\n\nThe same question should still be asked of go.mod, package.json and\npyproject.toml, which are manifests with their own analyzers.\n\nFIX (a) IS ALIGNMENT, NOT A NEW MODEL -- checked before proposing it.\n`defines_target` is registry-backed (edge_types.py:189, AXIS_RELATIONSHIP) and\nis ALREADY how other manifests express exactly this relationship:\n\n  xml_config.py:341   edge_type=\"defines_target\"     # entry points\n  noise_filter.py:25  \"...pyproject_script target (plus a `defines_target`\n                       edge into it)\"\n\nSo pyproject and xml already model manifest -> entrypoint as `defines_target`,\nand the mini-redis survey shows the rust manifest analyzer emitting BOTH\n`defines_target` (2) and `calls` (2) over the same Cargo.toml targets. The\n`calls` edges are the outlier, and they are the ones with the security\nconsequence."  # ac42
    fields: {}  # ac42
- op: discuss  # a5a9
  at: '2026-08-28T16:27:00Z'  # a5a9
  by: agent  # a5a9
  actor: jgstern_agent  # a5a9
  clock: 2  # a5a9
  nonce: a5a9  # a5a9
  message: "NO IMPACT FROM THIS SESSION, checked rather than assumed.\n\nNothing shipped 2026-08-28 added a `toml` catalogue or touched\n`_call_production_coverage`'s language branch, which is where this item lives\n(`languages_with_calls - set(catalogs)`). The disclosure work in PR #603\n(WI-fosir) re-ranked the UNCATALOGUED-MODULE and METHOD-STARVED lists; this\nitem's message comes from the separate blind-language branch and is unchanged.\n\nI DID CHECK A PLAUSIBLE INDIRECT INTERACTION AND IT DID NOT MATERIALISE.\nINV-nular F2 removed `TcpListener::bind` / `UdpSocket::bind` from rust's\nnet_recv, which left both modules method-only and therefore method-starved -- a\nNEW blocker sitting in the same six-early-return chain this item's note is about\n(\"a reason names the FIRST blocker only\"). The worry was that it would widen\nwithholding for rust repos, or mask/unmask this item's `toml` reason.\n\nA/B on aardvark-dns and firecracker, arms proven live by a canary on whether\n`std::net::TcpListener.bind` is present (OLD PRESENT / NEW ABSENT):\n  aardvark-dns  HOSTSECRET violated(4) -> violated(4); UNTRUSTED inconclusive(0)\n                -> inconclusive(0)\n  firecracker   HOSTSECRET violated(7) -> violated(7); UNTRUSTED inconclusive(0)\n                -> inconclusive(0)\nNo verdict and no evidence count moved. Two repos is not a survey, but the\nspecific interaction I suspected is not visible in it.\n\nSTILL P1 AND STILL THE SAME FIX: there is no toml.yaml among the fifteen shipped\ncatalogues, so a Cargo `[[bin]]` edge whose src language slot is `toml` still\nputs `toml` in the uncatalogued-language set and still withholds every verdict\non a rust binary crate."  # a5a9
